1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,240
And now, for you, Scoutmasters.

2
00:00:08,480 --> 00:00:12,320
Hello, and welcome to the Scoutmasters podcast. This is Clark Green.

3
00:00:12,580 --> 00:00:14,060
So, I was thinking about this.

4
00:00:15,380 --> 00:00:18,880
Whoever first said, where there's smoke, there's fire.

5
00:00:20,020 --> 00:00:24,020
There is very little chance that that person was a Scoutmaster.

6
00:00:24,900 --> 00:00:26,940
Right? I mean, think about it for a moment.

7
00:00:26,940 --> 00:00:30,560
Yeah. Yeah, very little chance.

8
00:00:31,160 --> 00:00:32,640
Hey, this is podcast number three.

9
00:00:56,940 --> 00:01:06,640
Hello, and welcome back. Good to have you.

10
00:01:07,060 --> 00:01:09,660
Thanks for listening, and thanks for sending me comments.

11
00:01:09,780 --> 00:01:17,560
I've gotten several since the first couple episodes of the podcast through Twitter and on the blog as comments.

12
00:01:17,560 --> 00:01:24,260
And I sure do appreciate that, and we'll have more information on how you can join in at the end of the podcast.

13
00:01:24,700 --> 00:01:27,760
This week, if you have a chance, I'd like you to go over and check out The Dump.

14
00:01:28,260 --> 00:01:29,580
No, not that dump.

15
00:01:29,780 --> 00:01:35,240
It's an online resource that some wonderful person up in Scouts Canada has set up.

16
00:01:35,740 --> 00:01:37,920
Now, they call themselves the Grumpy Ogre.

17
00:01:37,920 --> 00:01:48,980
What they've done is they've put together a site called The Dump, and it's thedump.scoutscan.com.

18
00:01:49,620 --> 00:01:57,120
And if you'll go check it out, what you'll find is just decades' worth of scouting booklets that were published by Scouts Canada

19
00:01:57,120 --> 00:02:05,280
and by the British Scouting Association, to name two, that have been laboriously scanned in and made into very readable PDF documents

20
00:02:05,280 --> 00:02:09,600
and are gathered together in this wonderful virtual library.

21
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:17,440
And it's especially valuable to us in the States because, you know, these little booklets didn't get a lot of distribution in the United States.

22
00:02:17,540 --> 00:02:22,020
So we have all that information now at our fingertips, and thank you, Grumpy Ogre.

23
00:02:22,300 --> 00:02:24,860
There'll be a link to The Dump on the blog, by the way.

24
00:02:24,860 --> 00:02:31,020
This episode, we are going to talk about, in Scout Mastership in seven minutes or less,

25
00:02:31,080 --> 00:02:33,500
we're going to talk about an approach to national policy.

26
00:02:34,400 --> 00:02:41,480
Then our third installment in our discussion of instructional methods for scouts.

27
00:02:42,160 --> 00:02:46,360
Oh, in between there, I have a story about a fellow Scout Master I think you'll enjoy.

28
00:02:46,640 --> 00:02:49,780
We'll wind up with a little moment of serendipity,

29
00:02:49,780 --> 00:02:55,260
especially when we think that our troop or our pack or our crew is just a terrible mess.

30
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:57,920
Because we have all been there.

31
00:02:58,200 --> 00:03:03,800
Today is February 8, 2010, and everybody knows what happens today.

32
00:03:03,920 --> 00:03:05,300
Is that right? Does everybody know?

33
00:03:05,300 --> 00:03:13,060
That looks great.

34
00:03:18,020 --> 00:03:21,560
It's the centenary of the Boy Scouts of America.

35
00:03:21,560 --> 00:03:30,860
Boy Scouts of America, 100 years old today.

36
00:03:31,480 --> 00:03:38,180
It all began very simply with an old war horse of a British soldier named Sir Robert Baden-Powell,

37
00:03:38,720 --> 00:03:39,360
who said,

38
00:03:39,360 --> 00:04:01,640
I mean, this idea is so simple,

39
00:04:01,640 --> 00:04:05,579
and just so direct and so adaptable

40
00:04:05,579 --> 00:04:09,380
that it would soon spread around the world

41
00:04:09,380 --> 00:04:13,220
and find its way into every corner of the world

42
00:04:13,220 --> 00:04:15,279
and nearly every country in the world

43
00:04:15,279 --> 00:04:19,380
because anybody could apply the principles of it.

44
00:04:20,979 --> 00:04:22,460
Baden-Powell also said,

45
00:04:22,580 --> 00:04:23,180
to an outsider,

46
00:04:23,340 --> 00:04:26,060
scouting must at first appear to be a very complex matter,

47
00:04:26,260 --> 00:04:29,900
and many a man is probably put off from becoming a Scout Master

48
00:04:29,900 --> 00:04:32,620
because of the enormous number and variety of things

49
00:04:32,620 --> 00:04:34,260
that he thinks he would have to know

50
00:04:34,260 --> 00:04:35,960
in order to teach his boys.

51
00:04:36,239 --> 00:04:37,620
But it need not be so

52
00:04:37,620 --> 00:04:40,039
if the man will only realize the following points.

53
00:04:40,280 --> 00:04:41,460
This is how simple it is.

54
00:04:41,479 --> 00:04:42,219
There's three things.

55
00:04:42,760 --> 00:04:44,919
The aim of scouting is quite a simple one.

56
00:04:46,159 --> 00:04:48,419
The Scout Master gives to the boy

57
00:04:48,419 --> 00:04:51,080
the ambition and desire to learn for himself

58
00:04:51,080 --> 00:04:53,960
by suggesting to him activities which attract him

59
00:04:53,960 --> 00:04:56,080
and which he pursues

60
00:04:56,080 --> 00:04:58,599
till he, by experience, does them right.

61
00:04:58,600 --> 00:05:02,720
Such activities are suggested in the book

62
00:05:02,720 --> 00:05:03,960
Scouting for Boys.

63
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:05,060
And number three,

64
00:05:06,560 --> 00:05:08,780
the Scout Master works through his patrol leaders.

65
00:05:09,500 --> 00:05:10,300
Both of these things,

66
00:05:10,380 --> 00:05:11,260
both of those little quotes

67
00:05:11,260 --> 00:05:14,140
are from Baden-Powell's aides to Scout Mastership,

68
00:05:14,220 --> 00:05:14,680
by the way.

69
00:05:16,220 --> 00:05:17,300
W.D. Boyce,

70
00:05:18,060 --> 00:05:19,540
a publisher and a businessman,

71
00:05:20,640 --> 00:05:22,460
discovered scouting,

72
00:05:23,100 --> 00:05:24,520
you know, quite by accident

73
00:05:24,520 --> 00:05:26,320
when he visited London

74
00:05:26,320 --> 00:05:28,440
and he brought it to the United States

75
00:05:28,440 --> 00:05:30,140
in 1910.

76
00:05:30,920 --> 00:05:32,420
And his newly minted

77
00:05:32,420 --> 00:05:33,800
Boy Scouts of America

78
00:05:33,800 --> 00:05:36,420
found a very eager following

79
00:05:36,420 --> 00:05:38,280
and quickly spread throughout the country.

80
00:05:39,640 --> 00:05:41,180
And a hundred years later,

81
00:05:41,320 --> 00:05:43,280
our future depends on

82
00:05:43,280 --> 00:05:46,500
an ever-renewed sense of relevance.

83
00:05:47,159 --> 00:05:48,280
And this is going to be

84
00:05:48,280 --> 00:05:49,180
one of those questions

85
00:05:49,180 --> 00:05:50,680
that we're going to hear asked

86
00:05:50,680 --> 00:05:52,800
many more than a hundred times

87
00:05:52,800 --> 00:05:53,600
over the coming year.

88
00:05:53,600 --> 00:05:56,060
Is the Boy Scouts of America still relevant?

89
00:05:56,520 --> 00:05:57,860
Is scouting still relevant

90
00:05:57,860 --> 00:05:59,680
in this day and age?

91
00:06:01,300 --> 00:06:03,260
Well, our Chief Scout Executive,

92
00:06:03,460 --> 00:06:05,720
Bob Mazzucca,

93
00:06:06,300 --> 00:06:06,920
said this.

94
00:06:06,960 --> 00:06:07,220
He said,

95
00:06:07,280 --> 00:06:08,120
over a hundred years,

96
00:06:08,200 --> 00:06:09,240
you've got a little arthritic

97
00:06:09,240 --> 00:06:10,080
and bureaucratic.

98
00:06:10,920 --> 00:06:12,360
We're steeped in tradition,

99
00:06:12,600 --> 00:06:13,740
which is a good thing.

100
00:06:14,460 --> 00:06:15,940
But we're not particularly good

101
00:06:15,940 --> 00:06:17,160
at innovation and renewal.

102
00:06:17,380 --> 00:06:19,140
We don't want to abandon tradition,

103
00:06:19,600 --> 00:06:20,940
but we want to be nimble.

104
00:06:20,940 --> 00:06:23,320
He then went on to say,

105
00:06:23,620 --> 00:06:25,140
we run the risk of becoming irrelevant

106
00:06:25,140 --> 00:06:26,820
if we don't adapt to things

107
00:06:26,820 --> 00:06:28,480
that attract kids today.

108
00:06:28,719 --> 00:06:31,040
But we run the risk of losing our way

109
00:06:31,040 --> 00:06:32,520
if we abandon the principles,

110
00:06:32,920 --> 00:06:34,200
which is the Scout law.

111
00:06:35,500 --> 00:06:36,800
So this week,

112
00:06:38,680 --> 00:06:40,280
somewhere around five million

113
00:06:40,280 --> 00:06:42,060
of us scouts and leaders

114
00:06:42,060 --> 00:06:43,200
will carry out

115
00:06:43,200 --> 00:06:45,600
this simple vital work

116
00:06:45,600 --> 00:06:46,900
by, you know,

117
00:06:46,980 --> 00:06:48,700
doing any one of a dozen things.

118
00:06:48,700 --> 00:06:49,599
We'll be at a meeting

119
00:06:49,599 --> 00:06:51,479
or we'll be on a camping trip

120
00:06:51,479 --> 00:06:52,719
or something like that.

121
00:06:52,880 --> 00:06:56,560
And it does us well to pause

122
00:06:56,560 --> 00:06:58,060
and remember we stand on the shoulders

123
00:06:58,060 --> 00:06:59,400
of a lot of people.

124
00:07:00,520 --> 00:07:02,500
And if you just think for a moment

125
00:07:02,500 --> 00:07:05,719
and you've been in scouting for a while,

126
00:07:05,840 --> 00:07:08,659
you know you can call the name

127
00:07:08,659 --> 00:07:09,880
of dozens of people

128
00:07:09,880 --> 00:07:11,960
who have helped you along the way.

129
00:07:11,960 --> 00:07:14,520
So to mark this centenary,

130
00:07:14,859 --> 00:07:17,060
let's go back to 1937.

131
00:07:17,700 --> 00:07:20,060
It is the 80th birthday

132
00:07:20,060 --> 00:07:23,460
of Robert Baden-Powell.

133
00:07:24,599 --> 00:07:27,200
And he makes this recording.

134
00:07:27,200 --> 00:07:31,320
My dear brother Scouts,

135
00:07:31,659 --> 00:07:33,320
I am 80 years old.

136
00:07:33,840 --> 00:07:34,800
What do you think of that?

137
00:07:35,340 --> 00:07:36,800
But I can't say that I feel

138
00:07:36,800 --> 00:07:38,780
very much older than some of you.

139
00:07:39,340 --> 00:07:40,740
I did a good lot of traveling,

140
00:07:41,159 --> 00:07:42,360
which opened my eyes

141
00:07:42,360 --> 00:07:43,320
to other countries

142
00:07:43,320 --> 00:07:44,480
and other peoples

143
00:07:44,480 --> 00:07:45,460
and their ways.

144
00:07:46,060 --> 00:07:47,140
I played games

145
00:07:47,140 --> 00:07:48,180
and got healthy

146
00:07:48,180 --> 00:07:50,020
and I did exploring

147
00:07:50,020 --> 00:07:51,760
and got lots of adventure.

148
00:07:52,800 --> 00:07:54,640
Yes, I thoroughly enjoyed my life.

149
00:07:54,640 --> 00:07:56,539
And though I had no money

150
00:07:56,539 --> 00:07:59,000
except what I earned by my work,

151
00:07:59,840 --> 00:08:01,099
I got lots of pleasure.

152
00:08:01,599 --> 00:08:02,500
But after a time,

153
00:08:02,599 --> 00:08:03,940
I found there was a difference

154
00:08:03,940 --> 00:08:05,960
between pleasure and happiness.

155
00:08:07,020 --> 00:08:08,419
Happiness is another thing.

156
00:08:09,099 --> 00:08:09,900
It is a joy

157
00:08:09,900 --> 00:08:11,659
which lasts with you always.

158
00:08:12,400 --> 00:08:13,500
And it comes largely

159
00:08:13,500 --> 00:08:15,580
not so much from amusing yourself

160
00:08:15,580 --> 00:08:18,120
as from doing good turns

161
00:08:18,120 --> 00:08:19,659
which help other people.

162
00:08:21,460 --> 00:08:23,960
80 years may seem to you a long time.

163
00:08:24,640 --> 00:08:26,320
But in all my 80 years,

164
00:08:26,640 --> 00:08:28,280
I can't remember a time

165
00:08:28,280 --> 00:08:29,599
when I wasn't busy.

166
00:08:30,400 --> 00:08:31,880
And so long as you are busy,

167
00:08:32,199 --> 00:08:33,799
you can't help being cheerful.

168
00:08:35,179 --> 00:08:37,000
So if ever you find yourself

169
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:38,100
without something to do,

170
00:08:38,520 --> 00:08:39,699
remember there are always

171
00:08:39,699 --> 00:08:41,380
lots of people wanting help

172
00:08:41,380 --> 00:08:42,740
who would be only too glad

173
00:08:42,740 --> 00:08:43,860
of a helping hand.

174
00:08:44,480 --> 00:08:45,720
If you go and help them

175
00:08:45,720 --> 00:08:46,880
and cheer them up,

176
00:08:47,580 --> 00:08:48,740
a funny thing happens.

177
00:08:49,480 --> 00:08:51,780
You find that by making others happy,

178
00:08:51,780 --> 00:08:53,280
you're making yourself

179
00:08:53,280 --> 00:08:54,600
all the happier too.

180
00:08:55,740 --> 00:08:57,100
I want you to have

181
00:08:57,100 --> 00:08:58,720
as long and jolly a life

182
00:08:58,720 --> 00:08:59,480
as I've had.

183
00:08:59,980 --> 00:09:01,079
And you can get it

184
00:09:01,079 --> 00:09:03,380
if you keep yourself healthy

185
00:09:03,380 --> 00:09:05,120
and helpful to others.

186
00:09:05,780 --> 00:09:07,720
I'll tell you my secret for this.

187
00:09:08,579 --> 00:09:10,459
I've always tried to carry out

188
00:09:10,459 --> 00:09:11,339
the Scout promise

189
00:09:11,339 --> 00:09:12,920
and the Scout law

190
00:09:12,920 --> 00:09:14,380
in all that I do.

191
00:09:14,380 --> 00:09:16,200
If you do that,

192
00:09:16,439 --> 00:09:17,380
you will make a success

193
00:09:17,380 --> 00:09:18,060
of your life

194
00:09:18,060 --> 00:09:20,420
and will have a very happy time

195
00:09:20,420 --> 00:09:22,420
even if you live to be 80

196
00:09:22,420 --> 00:09:23,200
like me.

197
00:09:24,100 --> 00:09:25,080
Thank you so much,

198
00:09:25,160 --> 00:09:25,600
Sir Robert.

199
00:09:26,360 --> 00:09:27,660
We're going to get back to work,

200
00:09:27,880 --> 00:09:28,900
keep ourselves busy

201
00:09:28,900 --> 00:09:30,240
here at the Scoutmaster Podcast

202
00:09:30,240 --> 00:09:32,800
and wish you a very fine centenary

203
00:09:32,800 --> 00:09:34,640
in this 100th year

204
00:09:34,640 --> 00:09:36,820
of the Boy Scouts of America.

205
00:09:36,820 --> 00:09:41,640
Scoutmastership in 7 minutes

206
00:09:41,640 --> 00:09:43,540
or less.

207
00:09:50,320 --> 00:09:52,540
Hey, we have a nationwide

208
00:09:52,540 --> 00:09:53,600
Scout movement

209
00:09:53,600 --> 00:09:55,000
in the Boy Scouts of America.

210
00:09:55,500 --> 00:09:56,640
2.8 million youth

211
00:09:56,640 --> 00:09:58,880
and 1.3 million adult participants.

212
00:09:59,660 --> 00:10:01,800
Right around 5 million people

213
00:10:01,800 --> 00:10:02,700
involved in Scouting

214
00:10:02,700 --> 00:10:03,560
in one way or another.

215
00:10:03,560 --> 00:10:06,060
And this whole movement

216
00:10:06,060 --> 00:10:08,319
is all about policies

217
00:10:08,319 --> 00:10:08,959
and procedures.

218
00:10:10,479 --> 00:10:11,339
Yeah, it is.

219
00:10:11,780 --> 00:10:12,439
Administering it

220
00:10:12,439 --> 00:10:13,219
is about policies

221
00:10:13,219 --> 00:10:13,819
and procedures.

222
00:10:13,959 --> 00:10:15,119
You may not like that idea,

223
00:10:15,420 --> 00:10:16,459
but it really is.

224
00:10:17,780 --> 00:10:19,739
And what I'm trying

225
00:10:19,739 --> 00:10:20,400
to get across

226
00:10:20,400 --> 00:10:23,359
in my thinking here

227
00:10:23,359 --> 00:10:25,099
is that sometimes

228
00:10:25,099 --> 00:10:27,819
we have practices

229
00:10:27,819 --> 00:10:28,859
and traditions

230
00:10:28,859 --> 00:10:31,479
that have gone

231
00:10:31,479 --> 00:10:32,599
off the rails

232
00:10:32,600 --> 00:10:34,200
in one way or another

233
00:10:34,200 --> 00:10:35,940
and are hurting

234
00:10:35,940 --> 00:10:38,040
your effectiveness

235
00:10:38,040 --> 00:10:39,780
as a Scout troop

236
00:10:39,780 --> 00:10:41,020
or a Venture crew

237
00:10:41,020 --> 00:10:41,940
or a Cub Pack.

238
00:10:42,120 --> 00:10:43,040
Let me give you an example.

239
00:10:44,300 --> 00:10:45,400
How many of you

240
00:10:45,400 --> 00:10:47,220
make a boy

241
00:10:47,220 --> 00:10:48,160
who is going

242
00:10:48,160 --> 00:10:49,280
to get the Scout badge

243
00:10:49,280 --> 00:10:49,880
memorize

244
00:10:49,880 --> 00:10:51,740
the Scout Oath

245
00:10:51,740 --> 00:10:52,280
and Law?

246
00:10:52,740 --> 00:10:53,280
Hands up.

247
00:10:53,820 --> 00:10:54,880
I see a lot of hands

248
00:10:54,880 --> 00:10:55,280
out there.

249
00:10:55,440 --> 00:10:55,980
I used to.

250
00:10:56,340 --> 00:10:57,200
I used to.

251
00:10:58,200 --> 00:10:59,000
Well, if you go

252
00:10:59,000 --> 00:10:59,860
and look at the requirement,

253
00:11:00,100 --> 00:11:00,900
the requirement says

254
00:11:00,900 --> 00:11:01,900
understand and agree

255
00:11:01,900 --> 00:11:02,579
to live by

256
00:11:02,579 --> 00:11:04,240
the Scout Oath

257
00:11:04,240 --> 00:11:04,740
and Promise

258
00:11:04,740 --> 00:11:05,439
and the Scout Law,

259
00:11:05,520 --> 00:11:05,800
the motto,

260
00:11:05,900 --> 00:11:06,260
the slogan,

261
00:11:06,340 --> 00:11:07,060
and the outdoor code.

262
00:11:07,120 --> 00:11:07,860
That's what it says.

263
00:11:07,920 --> 00:11:08,720
That's the requirement.

264
00:11:09,100 --> 00:11:10,040
It's very specific,

265
00:11:10,199 --> 00:11:10,600
isn't it?

266
00:11:11,079 --> 00:11:12,100
Understand and agree

267
00:11:12,100 --> 00:11:12,600
to live by.

268
00:11:12,640 --> 00:11:13,600
It does not say memorize.

269
00:11:14,340 --> 00:11:15,260
It does not say

270
00:11:15,260 --> 00:11:15,920
repeat from memory.

271
00:11:16,020 --> 00:11:17,040
That's in the

272
00:11:17,040 --> 00:11:18,000
Tenderfoot requirement.

273
00:11:18,840 --> 00:11:19,380
It says,

274
00:11:19,840 --> 00:11:20,980
the Tenderfoot requirement

275
00:11:20,980 --> 00:11:21,740
says repeat

276
00:11:21,740 --> 00:11:23,000
from memory

277
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:24,540
and explain

278
00:11:24,540 --> 00:11:25,579
in your own words

279
00:11:25,579 --> 00:11:26,420
the Scout Oath

280
00:11:26,420 --> 00:11:26,660
law,

281
00:11:26,740 --> 00:11:27,000
motto,

282
00:11:27,040 --> 00:11:27,500
and slogan.

283
00:11:27,500 --> 00:11:29,600
